{{also|pro-verb|Proverbs}}
==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*pro-}}
From {{uder|en|fro|proverbe}}, from {{uder|en|la|proverbium}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈpɹɒvɜːb/|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈpɹɑvɝb/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-proverb.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɜː(ɹ)b|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A commonly used sentence expressing popular wisdom.
#: {{cot|en|epigram|idiom|Thesaurus:saying}}
#: {{nearsyn|en|aphorism|maxim}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[striking]] or [[paradox]]ical assertion; an obscure [[saying]]; an [[enigma]]; a [[parable]].
#* {{RQ:KJV|John|16|29|passage=His disciples said unto him, Lo, now speakest thou plainly, and speakest no '''proverb'''.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A familiar illustration; a subject of contemptuous reference.
#* {{RQ:KJV|Deuteronomy|28|37|passage=Thou shalt become an astonishment, a '''proverb''', and a by word, among all nations.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[drama]] [[exemplify]]ing a proverb.

